title: Asbestosis and Asbestos-related Pleuro-Pulmonary Disease
---

# Abestosis and Abestos-related Pleuro-Pulmonary Disease

- **Asbestos** - a naturally-occurring silicate either chrysotile (straight) or serpentine (curved)
  - Favourable thermal and chemical insulation properties - incorporated in construction industries, especially in shipbuilding
  - Prolonged exposure leads to development of both pleural and pulmonary disease
- **Asbestosis:**
  - **Definition** - a diffuse interstitial lung disease (a pneumoconiosis) that requires prolonged, substantial exposure, and is rare w/ low-level or bystander exposure
  - **Pathophysiology of asbestosis:**
    - **Chronic inflammation and fibrosis** - due to direct toxicity against alveolar epithelium and alveolar macrophages resulting in release of cytokines
    - **Direct amputation of airways** - acini becomes simplified cystic spaces (bronchiolar columnar epithelium subsequently grows into cystic spaces)
    - **Combined effects:**
      - Diffuse interstitial fibrosis + amputation of airway –\> obliteration of airspaces and cystic dilatation of others (honeycombing of lungs)

      ![](../img/asbestosis_pathophysiology_1.png) 
      ![](../img/asbestosis_pathophysiology_2.png)
  - **Clinical presentation** - clinically presents as other ILD:
    - Hx - Distressing dry cough + chronic exertional breathlessness –\> prompts clinical presentation
    - P/E - clubbing (usually present), fine, late inspiratory crackles, initially heard at lower zones but becomes diffuse
    - Lung function test - restrictive pattern w/ reduced lung volume and gas transfer
    - HRCT - typical UIP pattern
  - **Diagnosis** - established on compatible clinical, radiological findings + +ve Hx of substantial asbestos exposure
  - **Prognosis** - slowly progressive, more indolent, and typically better prognosis than other UIPs:
    - Malignancy risk - 40% develop CA lung, 10% develop mesothelioma
    - Respiratory failure and cor-pulmonale - may develop in the rare severe cases
- **Pleural plaques, acute benign asbestos pleurisy, and diffuse pleural thickening**:
  - **Pleural plaques** - discrete circumscribed areas of hyaline fibrosis occuring at the parietal pleura of the chest wall, diaphragm, pericardium or mediastinum:
    - **Virtually asymptomatic** (benign lesions w/ no effects on lung functions) - only identified on CXR, CT thorax when particularly calcified

    
    ![](../img/asbestosis-related_benign_pleural_plaques.png)
  - **Acute benign asbestos pleurisy** - symptomatic pleurisy due to due to asbestos:
    - **Epidemiology** - occurs in 20% of asbestos workers but frequently under-reported
    - **Clinical presentation** - acute pleurisy + fever and systemic disturbances
    - **Diagnosis** - diagnosis by exclusion (r/o other causes of acute pleurisy and pleural effusion)
    - **Clinical significance** - repeated episodes bay follow the development of diffuse pleural thickening
  - **Diffuse pleural thickening** - diffuse (visceral) thickening of pleural causing **restrictive lung function impairment**: 
  ![](../img/diffuse_pleural_thickening.png)
- **Malignant pleural mesothelioma:**
  - **Definition** - malignant tumour affecting the pleura or less commonly the peritoneum, occurence suggestive of past asbestos exposure even at low levels
  - **Clinical presentation:**
    - **Progressive breathlessness** - results from pleural effusion
    - Unremitting chest pain - when there is involvement of the chest wall
    - Effects of invasion into lung parenchyma, pericardium and pericardium
  - **Prognosis** - invariably fatal:
    - Radical surgery in highly selected patients may offer a promise of cure
    - Majority of Tx is palliative
